{% doc %}
  Renders a "X% claimed" progress bar for a daily deal, from real inventory.

  There are two honest ways to count what has been claimed, and which one
  applies depends on who is holding the stock.

  DROPSHIP / COLLECTIVE (the normal case here). The supplier holds the
  inventory and it is enormous relative to the deal — a Collective product on
  this store carries 14,357 units. Inventory does not start at our allocation,
  so we snapshot it when the deal opens and measure the delta:

      claimed = starting_inventory - inventory on hand now
      percent = claimed / units_allocated

  That delta is real units sold while the deal was running.

  SELF-STOCKED. When no snapshot is recorded we assume the allocation IS the
  stock, which is true if you hold it yourself:

      claimed = units_allocated - inventory on hand now

  Renders NOTHING when it cannot be truthful:
    * no allocation set
    * the product does not track inventory in Shopify

  And nothing when the true number is not worth saying. The percentage is
  DISCLOSED, never adjusted — there is no floor, no rounding up, no minimum
  displayed figure. Either the real number shows or nothing does.

  Two gates, both on the real number:

    min_percent — below it the bar is hidden. "3% claimed" at 9am undersells a
    deal that has all day to run, and a shopper who reads it is worse informed
    than one who reads nothing.

    min_units — below this ALLOCATION the percentage never shows at all, at any
    value. On a 3-unit deal one sale is 33%, which is arithmetically true and
    tells the shopper something false about how fast it is going. Small
    allocations get the plain unit count instead, which is the honest form of
    the same fact.

  A caveat worth knowing: the supplier can restock mid-deal, which would make
  the delta understate what sold. It cannot overstate it, so the bar errs
  toward showing less urgency than is real — the safe direction.

  @param {object} product - The deal product.
  @param {number} units_allocated - Units committed to this deal. The divisor.
  @param {number} [starting_inventory] - Inventory on hand when the deal opened.
  @param {number} [min_percent] - Hide the bar below this true percentage.
  @param {number} [min_units] - Below this allocation, never show a percentage.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign allocated = units_allocated | plus: 0
  assign snapshot = starting_inventory | plus: 0
  assign tracked = false
  assign remaining = 0

  comment
    Sum across variants. A variant with inventory_management unset is untracked,
    and Shopify reports inventory_quantity 0 for those — counting it would read
    as "sold out" and wrongly push the bar to 100%.
  endcomment
  for variant in product.variants
    if variant.inventory_management == 'shopify'
      assign tracked = true
      if variant.inventory_quantity > 0
        assign remaining = remaining | plus: variant.inventory_quantity
      endif
    endif
  endfor

  if snapshot > 0
    assign claimed = snapshot | minus: remaining
  else
    assign claimed = allocated | minus: remaining
  endif

  comment
    Negative means stock went UP since the snapshot (a supplier restock), or the
    allocation is stale. Report nothing sold rather than a negative bar.
  endcomment
  if claimed < 0
    assign claimed = 0
  endif

  assign percent = 0
  if allocated > 0
    assign percent = claimed | times: 100 | divided_by: allocated
    if percent > 100
      assign percent = 100
    endif
  endif

  comment
    Units left in OUR allocation — not the supplier's shelf. On a dropship deal
    "Only 14,357 left" is absurd; "Only 12 left" is the number that is both
    true and meaningful, because the allocation is what we will honour.
  endcomment
  assign units_left = allocated | minus: claimed
  if units_left < 0
    assign units_left = 0
  endif

  comment
    Both gates default to 0 — an unset threshold is no threshold, so the
    snippet on its own still reports whatever is true.
  endcomment
  assign percent_gate = min_percent | plus: 0
  assign units_gate = min_units | plus: 0

  assign show_bar = false
  assign show_units_only = false

  if tracked and allocated > 0
    if allocated < units_gate
      comment
        Too few units for a percentage to mean anything. The unit count is the
        same fact without the false precision — and on an allocation this
        small it is the more useful one from the moment the deal opens.
      endcomment
      if units_left > 0 and units_left <= 25
        assign show_units_only = true
      endif
    elsif percent >= percent_gate
      assign show_bar = true
    endif
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  The stylesheet stays unconditional. The section's own .aj-deal__* rules live
  in this file and reach it through whichever of these snippets renders.

  Below either gate the MARKUP renders NOTHING — no wrapper, no empty track, no
  reserved height. A container held open for a bar that is not there reads as
  a broken page, which is a worse answer than saying nothing.
{%- endcomment -%}
{{ "aj-deal.css" | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{%- if show_bar or show_units_only -%}
  <div class="aj-claimed">
    {%- if show_bar -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        aria-hidden, not role="progressbar". The sentence underneath states the
        same figure, so announcing the bar as well made a screen reader read
        "30%" twice. The bar is the redundant presentation of a fact that is
        already in text — which is also what lets it be treated as decorative
        rather than as a graphic someone must be able to see.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="aj-claimed__track" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="aj-claimed__fill" style="width: {{ percent }}%;"></div>
      </div>
      <p class="aj-claimed__text">
        <strong>{{ percent }}% claimed</strong>
        {%- if units_left > 0 and units_left <= 25 -%}
          <span class="aj-claimed__remaining">Only {{ units_left }} left</span>
        {%- endif -%}
      </p>
    {%- else -%}
      <p class="aj-claimed__text">
        <strong>Only {{ units_left }} left</strong>
      </p>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
